.TH BLINK 8 "2026-11-12" "blink" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
blink \- flash a host LED to identify the physical machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo blink
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RI [ seconds ]
.br
.B blink
.RB \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B blink
flashes a host LED for the specified number of seconds, displaying a countdown.
It is intended to help identify a Raspberry Pi in a rack or stack.
The default duration is 20 seconds.
.PP
The command searches
.I /sys/class/leds
for a supported LED, preferring known power LED names.
If no known name matches, it searches for a name containing
.B power
and then falls back to the first available LED.
.PP
The LED is switched on and off at half-second intervals.
Its original brightness is restored on completion or interruption by SIGINT
or SIGTERM.
Root privileges are required to control the LED.
Displaying help does not require root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show host information, the selected LED, and restoration messages in addition
to the countdown.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.I seconds
Duration in seconds, specified as a positive integer. Default: 20.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sudo blink 5
Flash the LED for five seconds.
.TP
.B sudo blink \-v 30
Flash for thirty seconds with verbose output.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/class/leds/*/brightness
Linux LED brightness controls used by the command.
.SH NOTES
Available LEDs and their behaviour depend on the hardware and operating system.
The selected LED may be an activity LED when no power LED is found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudo (8)
